          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do I need an antibiotic every time?",
          "answer": "Antibiotics are important medicines, but they are not always needed and do not work for all infections. Knowing when to use them is key to protecting your health and preventing antibiotic resistance.\n\n*   **Viral Infections (like the common cold, most sore throats, acute bronchitis, and flu):** Antibiotics are *not* effective against viruses. For these common illnesses, antibiotics will not make you better, do not prevent complications, and can cause harm. Harms include side effects like stomach upset, allergic reactions, disruption of the body's natural helpful bacteria, and contributing to the problem of antibiotic resistance. For these conditions, supportive care, such as rest, fluids, and over-the-counter symptom relief, is usually recommended.\n\n*   **Bacterial Infections:** Antibiotics are designed to treat infections caused by bacteria. When a bacterial infection is diagnosed, antibiotics can be very effective.\n\n*   **Urinary Tract Infections (UTIs):** For uncomplicated lower UTIs (cystitis), there is some discussion about whether immediate antibiotics are always necessary. Some guidance suggests that some mild cases might resolve without antibiotics, and a \"wait and see\" approach with symptom management could be an option. However, other guidance still recommends immediate antibiotics to help with symptoms and prevent the infection from spreading to the kidneys. For recurrent UTIs, antibiotics may sometimes be used as a preventative measure, but this is carefully considered due to the risk of resistance.\n\nTherefore, you do not need an antibiotic every time you are sick, especially if the illness is caused by a virus. A healthcare professional can help determine if your infection is bacterial and if an antibiotic is the right treatment.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n[sources: guideline-uri-antibiotic-stewardship, condition-upper-respiratory-infection, guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals, condition-influenza, guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment]",
